aba04bcfc2d8a9238793b08f8c795e2d59a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-72831" :question/id #uuid "f824808a-65d2-53a3-8fad-f1c4f0373d54" :question/math-academy-id "q-72831" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952589/assets/23b4f6296c9e840ad90b8235fa66f17e767c61a6cfeac5bc9566fd1fbd35b004.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the line $y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$ Find, in radians, the $x$-coordinate of the point $A.$\n\n$$\n\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two equations gives:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\n Now, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ To get a value that's inside the required domain, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\pi +{x}_{1}=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ ." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-72831/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a71da663-e7a4-5781-973c-5dc7e4deb83f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-72831"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
